  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on adjusting the weights of outcomes with probabilities less than 20%, but in this problem, only Lottery A has an outcome at exactly 20% (not less), so no adjustment applies. The individual, however, explicitly considers the 20% chance of no loss and is influenced by it—specifically because it offers a chance to avoid loss altogether. While both the reason and the individual pay attention to the 20% outcome, the individual does not describe any adjustment of probability weights or a comparison based on weighted sums of small-probability outcomes. Instead, the justification is based on the mere existence of a no-loss possibility and the insignificance of the monetary difference between the losses. This suggests a focus on outcome valence (avoiding loss) and outcome similarity, not on a formal probability weighting rule. Therefore, although the individual considers a low-probability outcome, they do not apply the specific rule of the decision reason (adjusting weights for probabilities <20%). The reasoning process described is qualitatively different from the decision reason’s mechanism.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
